---
/** How active your whole day is, and how much of that number to trust. Rendered by /science/movement/activity-level; see scienceTopics.ts. */
import VideoSlot from "../../components/VideoSlot.astro";
---

<div class="mt-6"><VideoSlot topic="activity-level" /></div>


<div class="mt-6 space-y-4 text-sm leading-relaxed text-text-secondary">
  <p>
    <strong class="text-text-primary">Physical Activity Level</strong> measures how active your
    entire day is, not just your workouts. It is your total daily energy expenditure divided by
    your basal metabolic rate — the energy your body would use lying still. A PAL of 1.0 would
    mean complete bed rest. Around 2.5 is elite-athlete territory. Verve reads it from Apple
    Health's basal and active energy, averaged over seven days, and from your profile's
    estimated basal rate when your wearable writes none.
  </p>

  <h3 id="the-four-levels" class="scroll-mt-8 vrv-anchor pt-2 text-lg font-semibold text-text-primary">The four levels</h3>
  <p>
    The tiers come from the Institute of Medicine's Dietary Reference Intakes, which defined
    them to set energy requirements for populations: sedentary 1.00–1.39, low active 1.40–1.59,
    active 1.60–1.89, very active 1.90–2.50.
  </p>

  <h3 id="read-the-trend-not-the-number" class="scroll-mt-8 vrv-anchor pt-2 text-lg font-semibold text-text-primary">Read the trend, not the number</h3>
  <p>
    Both halves of this ratio are estimates, so the error compounds. Active calories come from
    your wrist, where systematic reviews put energy-expenditure error above 30%, and they miss
    effort that does not move your arm — cycling, rowing, lifting, carrying. Your resting figure
    is not measured at all; it is predicted from your height, weight, age and sex. Every wearable
    tested against doubly-labelled water <em>under</em>-estimates, for Apple Watch by roughly
    394 kcal a day, and the bias grows at higher effort and higher body fat.
  </p>
  <p>
    The practical consequence: a single PAL carries a band wide enough to straddle two tiers.
    Whether it drifts up over months does not. That is why Verve shows this as a trend read
    against the tier bands rather than as a figure to hit, and why the app's chart marks which
    band each week or month fell in rather than asking you to read a slope off a noisy line.
  </p>

  <h3 id="reading-the-page" class="scroll-mt-8 vrv-anchor pt-2 text-lg font-semibold text-text-primary">Reading the page in the app</h3>
  <p>
    Each lens shows two figures first: the period's PAL and its active energy a day. The strip
    under them is the four bands, with the reading marked on it. <em>Day</em> shows the PAL of the
    last seven days rather than today's: a single day's ratio swings with one walk or one rest
    day, and a part-day is not yet a day. Today's own figure is the active energy, hour by hour,
    read against your usual by the same time of day, the median of the last 28 days up to the
    same minute. <em>Week</em> is the last seven days a bar a day, the dashed line its average.
    <em>Month</em> and <em>Year</em> are the last five weeks and the last twelve months, each bar a
    period's active energy a day, coloured by that period's own band, with its PAL above it; each
    is read against the same span before it.
  </p>
  <p>
    What PAL is made of: the energy your body uses at rest, predicted rather than measured; the
    active energy your watch estimates; and the energy of digesting food, which Health does not
    report and Verve adds back (below). Active energy a day is the period's active energy over
    its days with a resting reading, so a day the watch was off does not dilute the average.
  </p>
  <p>
    Only an Apple Watch writes resting energy to Health. A Whoop, an Oura ring or a Garmin
    writes active energy and no resting figure, so the ratio had no denominator and the page
    stayed empty for anyone wearing one. When a window has fewer than three days of resting
    energy, Verve now estimates your basal metabolic rate from the height, weight, age and sex
    on your profile with the Mifflin-St Jeor equation (Mifflin et al, <em>Am J Clin Nutr</em>
    1990), the equation a 2005 systematic review found within 10% of measured resting
    metabolic rate in more adults than Harris-Benedict, Owen or the WHO equation, multiplies it
    by the days the window has active energy for, and computes PAL by the same formula. Health's
    own resting figure is itself an equation over the same inputs, so this swaps one estimate
    for another rather than a measurement for an estimate; even so, the app says
    <em>BMR estimated from your profile</em> wherever it shows such a figure, and measured
    resting energy wins whenever there is enough of it.
  </p>
  <p id="per-day-denominator" class="scroll-mt-8">
    Since 30 October 2026 that choice is made a day at a time, not a window at a time. Each day in
    a window has its own denominator: Health's resting energy for that day when it wrote one, the
    profile's BMR when it did not. The window used to sum active energy over seven days and divide
    by the five days that had a resting figure, which inflated both the kilocalories a day and the
    PAL whenever a second device wrote energy on days the watch was off. A window now reads
    <em>BMR estimated</em> when every day took the profile's figure, says <em>resting energy from
    your profile on N of 7 days</em> when some did, and says nothing when none did.
  </p>
  <p id="workout-energy-only" class="scroll-mt-8">
    One device gets no PAL at all: a Whoop. Whoop writes one active-energy sample per started
    workout, about 30 minutes long, and nothing for the rest of the day, on iPhone and on Android
    alike (four samples in four days on both phones, each a session, 30 October 2026). A day's
    workouts are not a day's energy; built on them, the ratio read 1.22, &ldquo;Sedentary&rdquo;,
    for a week with 123 aerobic minutes. So when every writer of active energy in the window is
    workout-shaped (its samples run ten minutes or more and it writes at most four a day), Verve
    withholds PAL, shows a dash with the reason, and labels the kilocalories <em>workout
    energy</em> on the Activity row, on this page's lenses, in the backed-up daily signals and on
    this site. A watch that writes energy through the day beside the Whoop restores it.
  </p>

  <p>
    On the website, My health shows the same two figures, activity level and active energy,
    one a day, as far back as Apple Health or Health Connect holds your energy: up to five years.
    They arrive only while <em>Back up watch readings</em> is on in the app, under Settings, Your
    data, Backup; the Months and Years views there read each period against the same four bands.
  </p>

  <h3 id="the-one-correction-verve-makes" class="scroll-mt-8 vrv-anchor pt-2 text-lg font-semibold text-text-primary">The one correction Verve makes</h3>
  <p>
    Apple reports basal and active energy but not the thermic effect of food — the energy spent
    digesting, conventionally about 10% of total daily expenditure. Their sum is therefore not
    total daily energy expenditure, so Verve divides by 0.9 to recover it. That correction is
    definitional rather than a judgement call, and it is the only adjustment applied.
  </p>
  <p>
    Two further adjustments were tried and removed: an additive correction for occupational
    activity, and a rule that raised the tier when logged training contradicted a sedentary
    label. Both were judgement calls stacked on a base with more than 30% error, and the
    occupational one double-counted part of the very bias the thermic-effect correction already
    approximates. Layers of inference do not rescue a noisy instrument; they only obscure how
    noisy it is.
  </p>
</div>
